[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mNext: how notifications reach people.[0m This is the infrastructure fork — it decides whether we need background jobs, an
  email provider, and service workers, or none of that.

  - [3mIn-app only[0m — a bell icon with an unread count and a notification list. Everything stays in the app's own datastore;
    no external services, no deliverability problems, no secrets to manage. But nothing reaches anyone who isn't
    looking at the tab.
  - [3mIn-app + email[0m — same in-app feed, plus email for anything unread after a delay. This is what makes notifications
    actually work for people who aren't in the app all day. Costs an email provider, a background job runner, per-user
    email preferences, and unsubscribe handling.
  - [3mIn-app + web push[0m — browser notifications via service worker and the Push API. Feels immediate and needs no
    third-party provider, but requires HTTPS, a permission prompt most users decline, and VAPID key management — and it
    still reaches nobody with the browser closed.

  I'd recommend starting in-app only, with the delivery layer written as a dispatcher behind an interface so email is a
  second implementation rather than a retrofit. In-app is the substrate every other channel reads from anyway — you need
  the notification records regardless — and it's the only piece that's useless to defer. Adding email later is additive
  if the dispatcher seam exists from day one; that seam is cheap now and awkward to introduce afterward.
